A||||5||contra||Poor handling...need help...||||9||
Z||000000||contra||09-16-2000||10:20 AM||||My fiero's both have tight handling and i love the way they corner.<P>I'm noticing though on my GT especially that at speeds of 120km/h or higher the handling gets really rough and almost to the point where i can feel the steering wheel vibrate alot and the steering is really sensitive to bumps.<P>A) i know i shouldn't be driving at those speeds to begin with<P>b) i need an alignment so it might be that but i want advice anyways<P>c) could it be my suspension? like shock or springs? my shocks look pretty rusty and need to be changed i think<P>d) could it be my tires?<P>e) anything else??<P>I am thinking of upgrading to either poly bushings or metal bushings<BR>any thoughts? how hard is it to accomplish for me? (i'm 18 and still sort of new to cars in general)<P>I dont know if i want to spend money on shocks or anything yet  because i want to lower my car once i get rims for it. so i'm not too keen on spending money of suspension then having to change it again later. i've never lowered a car before, do they need to change the shocks and springs? or just springs? if it's just springs, maybe i should get new shocks in the meantime. any suggestions?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ok i've asked enough for today<BR>heh<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||09-16-2000||10:52 AM||||you can check the shock on the steering rack for leaking, it may be the original. both of my notchback SE's did that an so does a friend of mine's 87 GT. Though my SE's start acting up about 85-90 MPH the 87 GT i drove didnt start until 100-110. does it happen in bumpy turns? it could be bump steer.||||reg||1
Z||000002||contra||09-16-2000||12:13 PM||||nope<BR>it's generally pretty good on bumps<BR>mind you i realize i'll notice bumps if there is bumps on the road<BR>but the shaky steering wheel on the highway can get pretty disturbing because it feels unsafe. i'll check the shock<BR>any clues as to how to do that?<BR>would i need to replace something or just tighten something...hopefully not too expensive if i have to replace it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-16-2000||12:22 PM||||Shocks, bushings, alignment, ball joints, tires, worn rack, etc.... any of that could be a problem. No way to tell w/o inspecting everything. Could even be rear arms or cradle bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||09-16-2000||03:09 PM||||Check shocks by pushing down hard on each fender.  The car should bounce once and then stop.  Check tires for "cupping" which also shows bouncing, worn out shocks.  Check visually for oil leaks coming from shocks.  Did you see rust, or was it actually oil streaks on your shocks?  I'd seriously consider replacing the shocks if they're bad, for a variety of reasons, especially safety (yours and mine).  <P>My car vibrated at about 80 mph.  That's a typical symptom of out-of-balance wheels/tires.  A balance job fixed that one pretty easily. <P>When you go "at speed" the steering lightens up, but check your tires for over-inflation.  <P>Poly bushings will make your car ride rougher, which you're already complaining about, so that might not be a good move, and metal, forget it.  That's for racing and masochists.  <P>Bump steer is different than feeling bumps.  Fieros are noted for bump steer and you describe that characteristic.<P>Theogre's stuff is also safety related and you should look into them. All of our cars are getting on in years now and things wear out.<BR> <BR>Safety before appearance.  <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 09-16-2000).]||||reg||1
